# target-release.env — the SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for the Debian release the
# device image targets.
#
# suite-literal-ok(file): this file's own prose names the retired suite and its
# VERSION_ID on purpose — it is the record of what the mapping replaced, and the
# only place in the tree where saying `bookworm` is the point rather than a bug.
#
# Before this file existed the same two facts — the suite name and the os-release
# VERSION_ID — were spelled out independently in the orchestrator, the mkosi
# top-level config, the runtime postinst, the device apt-source writer, the
# sysext backend, the add-on builder, the add-on descriptors, the add-on schema,
# the manifest validator and a board-preflight fixture. A release bump therefore
# meant a ten-site edit whose only failure signal was an image that BUILDS and
# BOOTS and is simply wrong: an apt source pointing at the previous suite, or a
# sysext the kernel silently refuses to merge because its VERSION_ID no longer
# matches the host os-release. Every one of those sites now derives from here.
#
# FORMAT: one `: "${KEY:=value}"` line per key — a bash DEFAULT-ASSIGNMENT
# fragment, not a plain KEY=value list, and the difference is load-bearing:
#
#   * An already-set environment variable WINS. That is what lets the build
#     environment stay authoritative and what lets a scratch experiment or a test
#     override one value without editing this file.
#   * The derived suites below expand `${RELEASE}`, so an override of RELEASE
#     re-derives them. A plain `APT_SUITE_UPDATES=trixie-updates` would keep
#     saying `trixie-updates` under `RELEASE=bookworm` — a silent inconsistency
#     between the suite the rootfs is bootstrapped from and the suites its apt
#     sources name.
#
# Keep it free of anything a five-line parser cannot read: no arrays, no
# conditionals, no `$(...)`, and no key whose default is not a bare token.
#
# HOW EACH CONSUMER REACHES IT
#   * Host-side shell (lib/, ci/, mkosi/app/) — `source` the reader
#     lib/shared/target-release-lib.sh and call `target_release_load`.
#   * The one Python consumer (ci/validate-manifests.py) parses these lines with
#     the same top-down `${KEY}` expansion rule.
#   * mkosi SUBIMAGE CHROOTS (mkosi.images/runtime/mkosi.postinst.chroot,
#     mkosi/customize/apt-ceralive-repo.sh) — this file is NOT mounted there, so
#     they read the forwarded RELEASE environment variable, which lib/orchestrate.sh
#     seeds from here and forwards through env_names + mkosi.conf PassEnvironment=.
#     They fail closed when it is absent; there is deliberately no literal suite
#     fallback, because a stale default is exactly the failure this file exists to
#     prevent.
#   * mkosi's own `[Distribution] Release=` — mkosi expands `${VAR}` only in
#     PATH-typed settings, never in `Release=` (verified against the pinned mkosi
#     26: a `Release=${RELEASE}` survives into `mkosi summary` verbatim). The
#     orchestrator therefore passes `--release "${RELEASE}"` on the CLI as the
#     authoritative plumb, and mkosi.conf keeps a mirrored literal that
#     ci/check-suite-literals.sh fails over if it ever disagrees with RELEASE below.
#
# THE AUDIT GATE: ci/check-suite-literals.sh scans every production file for a
# `bookworm` / `VERSION_ID=12` literal and for a mirror that has drifted from this
# file. Run it after any edit here.
#
# ARMBIAN_SUITE IS NOT THIS. The Armbian BSP apt suite (lib/orchestrate.sh
# ARMBIAN_SUITE, manifests/armbian-bsp-deb-versions.txt) names the archive the
# kernel/DTB/U-Boot/firmware `.deb`s are fetched from. It is BSP-deb provenance
# and is orthogonal to the userspace suite below: Armbian may keep publishing the
# RK35xx vendor BSP under an older suite long after the rootfs has moved on, and
# collapsing the two would silently repoint the boot stack at a suite that does
# not carry it. Never derive one from the other.
#
# shellcheck shell=bash

# Debian suite the rootfs is built from. Feeds mkosi's `--release`, the device's
# own deb822 apt sources, and the build-time bootstrap-source cleanup.
: "${RELEASE:=trixie}"

# The os-release VERSION_ID that suite ships. This is the sysext MERGE KEY: the
# kernel refuses to merge an extension whose extension-release VERSION_ID does not
# match the running host, so it must track RELEASE exactly. Also the `{os_version}`
# axis of the add-on R2 delivery path (addons/{os_version}/{board}/{feature}.raw).
: "${OS_VERSION_ID:=13}"

# Derived apt suites. Spelled out rather than suffixed at each call site so a
# release whose security/updates suites are NOT plain `<suite>-security` /
# `<suite>-updates` can be expressed here instead of by editing three writers.
: "${APT_SUITE:=${RELEASE}}"
: "${APT_SUITE_UPDATES:=${RELEASE}-updates}"
: "${APT_SUITE_SECURITY:=${RELEASE}-security}"
